From e0723b3f9691e9c322aafaa21de060bcece737a8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Avery Felts <averyfelts@aol.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 08:27:38 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 182/188] ntdll, msvcrt: NEON string and memory functions on
 ARM64 and ARM64EC.

strlen, wcslen, memchr, memcmp, strchr, strcmp and memmove/memcpy in ntdll
and in msvcrt, ucrtbase and the msvcr* DLLs built from it. x86_64 programs
reach these through ARM64EC, where the C loops ran a byte at a time (the
x86_64 build gets sse2_memmove). The shared versions are in
include/wine/neon_string.h:
- Scans that stop at a terminator read aligned 16-byte blocks, so a load
  never crosses into the next page, and mask the bytes before the start.
- strcmp reads both strings 16 bytes at a time while neither block crosses
  a page, then a byte at a time.
- memmove copies 32 bytes per step with the head or tail block loaded first,
  so overlapping copies in either direction are correct, and aligns the
  destination for long copies when the buffers are 64 bytes or more apart.

Native benchmark against the C versions (ns, old -> new):
  strlen 128/1024 bytes 36 -> 3, 375 -> 33; wcslen 35 -> 11, 382 -> 83
  memchr 51 -> 5, 304 -> 20; memcmp 82 -> 5, 365 -> 21; strcmp 46 -> 6
  memmove: 2-6x below 64 bytes, 3x on misaligned 4 KB, equal when aligned.
Through FEX from x86_64: strlen(1024) 240 -> 18 ns.
